{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Thus says your servant Jacob: With Laban I have sojourned, and I have lingered until now.</b> Rashi explained: גַּרְתִּי — I did not become a prince or notable, but a sojourner. Another matter: גַּרְתִּי — six hundred and thirteen commandments I observed. And these two explanations contradict one another: for if he said to him, 'I did not become a prince,' if so, he comes before him in submission. And if he said to him, 'Six hundred and thirteen commandments I observed,' the implication is the opposite — that he sent him word that he does not fear him, since he observed six hundred and thirteen commandments and is not included in 'and it shall come to pass, when you shall break loose, that you shall shake his yoke from upon your neck' (Genesis 27:40). And it is further difficult why he called him 'my lord' and himself 'servant,' and the rest of the gestures of submission that he made toward him. And one may further wonder at the language of the verse, that it would express six hundred and thirteen in the phrase 'גַּרְתִּי.'" "Therefore my heart tells me that these two explanations both demonstrate a single truth together — not as Rashi explained 'another matter,' which implies that each is an independent explanation. And this is the explanation of the matter: Jacob sent to him, saying, 'With Laban I have sojourned' (Genesis 32:5) — I did not become a prince or notable, but a sojourner — to tell him that the blessings were not fulfilled in me. And he did not come to contradict his father and to make his blessings a fraud, heaven forbid, but rather he said this: when my father blessed me, he thought I was Esau, and on that understanding he blessed me. And the deception that I practiced availed me nothing, for 'the deceitful man does not roast his prey' (Proverbs 12:27) — the food with which he fed his father in order to obtain the blessings through it. And no man touches what is prepared for his fellow, and all the blessings follow the intention of my father, and in his mind they were meant to alight upon the head of Esau, and so it shall be. And the proof for this is that indeed 'a sojourner am I in the land' — not a prince or notable. And if you would say: surely the blessings will yet be fulfilled in me, and the reason I did not become a prince or notable until this day is because I did not keep the Torah — for thus was the condition stipulated at the time the blessings were given, on condition that I observe six hundred and thirteen commandments, as it says, 'and it shall come to pass, when you shall break loose, that you shall shake his yoke' etc. (Genesis 27:40) — to this he said 'גַּרְתִּי,' behold six hundred and thirteen commandments I observed, and even so I did not become a prince or notable. Rather, it is certain that my deeds were of no avail, for deception does not avail the deceiver through his deception, and the blessings will alight upon the head of the one whom Isaac intended at the time the blessings were spoken. Therefore he said 'גַּרְתִּי,' which encompasses both: the idea of sojourning, and the idea of six hundred and thirteen — to tell you that even though six hundred and thirteen commandments I observed, nevertheless 'a sojourner am I in the land.' For were it not so, why did he say 'גַּרְתִּי'? He should have said 'עָבַדְתִּי' — I served — for in truth he was a servant to Laban, and that would indicate even more strongly the opposite of the blessing 'be a master over your brothers' (Genesis 27:29). Rather, it was specifically in order to infer from the phrase 'גַּרְתִּי' the letters תרי״ג. And were I not afraid of my companions, I would erase 'another matter' from Rashi's explanation, for in truth the two plain senses require one another."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:31.168446225Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}